nodes.statement:
~{\it Theorem~ef{main.thm}}. Let $u$ be a solution of \eqref{inls} such that $\|u\|_{L^2}=M_\mathrm{gs}$ and $\lim_{t\uparrow T} H(u) =+\infty$. \medskip 
oindent {\em Step~1.} Let $\{t_n\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}} \subset \mathbb{R}$ be a sequence of times such that $t_n\uparrow T$ as $nightarrow\infty$. We set \[ u_n=u(t_n), \quad \lambda_n^2=H(u_n), \quad v_n(x)=\lambda_n^{-N/2}u_n(\lambda_n^{-1}x). \] We note that $\lambda_nightarrow\infty$ as $nightarrow\infty$, and $\|v_n\|_{L^2}=\|u_n\|_{L^2}=\|u_0\|_{L^2}=M_\mathrm{gs}$ by the $L^2$ scaling. Furthermore, \[  egin{split} H(v_n)=\lambda_n^{-2}H(u_n)=1. \end{split} \] On the other hand, by conservation of the energy, \[  egin{split} E(v_n)&= rac12\lambda_n^{-2}H(u_n)- rac{1}{p+1}\lambda_n^{-2}\int_{\mathbb{R}^N} |u_n(y)|^{p+1}dy\ &=\lambda_n^{-2} E(u_n)=\lambda_n^{-2}E(u_0)	o 0 \quad 	ext{as} \ n	o\infty. \end{split} \] Hence, by Proposition ef{compact.prop}, there exist $Q\in\mathcal{G}$, $\gamma_0\in\mathbb{R}$ and $\lambda_0>0$ such that, up to a subsequence,  egin{equation} \lim_{nightarrow\infty} \|v_n-e^{i\gamma_0}Q_{\lambda_0}\|_{H^1}=0, \end{equation} where $Q_{\lambda_0}(x)=\lambda_0^{N/2}Q(\lambda_0 x)$. \medskip 
oindent {\em Step~2.} We now prove that $u_n$ concentrates all of its mass at $x=0$ as $nightarrow\infty$. We show that, in the sense of distributions, \[ |u_n|^2ightarrow M_\mathrm{gs}^2\delta_0. \] Indeed, for any $\varphi\in C^\infty_0(\mathbb{R}^N)$, using the change of variables $y=\lambda_n^{-1}x$ we find that \[  egin{split} \int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|u_n(y)|^2\varphi(y)dy &=\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|u_n(\lambda_n^{-1}x)|^2\varphi(\lambda^{-1}_nx)\lambda_n^{-N}dx =\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|v_n(x)|^2\varphi(\lambda_n^{-1}x)dx\ &=\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}(|v_n(x)|^2-|Q(x)|^2)\varphi(\lambda_n^{-1}x)dx+\int_{\mathbb{R}^N} |Q(x)|^2\varphi(0)dx\ &\phantom{\leqslant} \ +\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|Q(x)|^2(\varphi(\lambda_n^{-1}x)-\varphi(0))dx. \end{split} \] Thus, we obtain \[  egin{split} \Big|\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|u_n(y)|^2\varphi(y)dy -M_\mathrm{gs}^2\varphi(0)\Big| &\leqslant \|\varphi\|_{L^\infty}\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}||v_n(x)|^2-|Q(x)|^2|dx\ &\phantom{\leqslant} \ +\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|Q(x)|^2|\varphi(\lambda_n^{-1}x)-\varphi(0)|dx. \end{split} \] We conclude this step by noticing that $|v_n|^2$ converges strongly to $|Q|^2$ in $L^1(\mathbb{R}^N)$ from (ef{b}), so the first integral vanishes as $nightarrow\infty$. Since $\lambda_nightarrow\infty$ the second integral also converges to zero by the dominated convergence theorem. Hence, we obtain  egin{equation} \int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|u_n(y)|^2\varphi(y)dyightarrow M_\mathrm{gs}^2\varphi(0). \end{equation} \medskip 
oindent {\em Step~3.} We now show that $u(t)\in\Sigma$ for all $t\in[0,T)$. Let $\phi\in C^\infty_0(\mathbb{R}^N)$ be a radial and non-negative function such that $\phi(x)=|x|^2$ for $|x|\leqslant 1$. It is easy to see that there exists a constant $C>0$ such that \[ |
abla \phi (x) |^2\leqslant C\phi(x) \] for all $x\in\mathbb{R}^N$. Indeed, since $\phi$ is radially symmetric we may choose $f\in C(\mathbb{R},\mathbb{R}_+) $, such that $\phi(|x|)=f(|x|)$. By Taylor's formula, for all $r\in\mathbb{R}$ and $h\in\mathbb{R}$ there exists $y\in[r,r+h]$, such that \[ 0\leqslant f(r+h)=f(r)+f'(r)h+ rac{f''(y)}{2}h^2\leqslant f(r)+f'(r)h+C'h^2, \] where $C'=1+\max_{r\in\mathbb{R}} rac{|f'(r)|}{2}>0$. Since the right-hand side is a positive quadratic polynomial, we must have that $|f'(r)|^2-4C'f(r)\leqslant 0$, thus $|f'(r)|^2\leqslant Cf(r)$, where $C=4C'>0$. For all $R>0$, we define $\phi_R(x)=R^2\phi(x/R)$, and for all $t\in[0,T)$, \[ \Gamma_R(t)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}\phi_R(x)|u(t,x)|^2dx. \] A direct calculation, similar to the proof of Lemma~ef{virial.lemma}, yields that \[ \Gamma_R'(t)=2	ext{Re}\int\phi_R ar{u}\partial_tu =-2	ext{Im}\int\phi_R ar{u}(\Delta u+ rac{c}{|x|^2}u+|u|^{p-1}u) =2\int
abla\phi_R\cdot	ext{Im}( ar{u}
abla u). \] Since $\|u\|_{L^2}=\|Q\|_{L^2}$, we can apply Lemma ef{banica.lem} and the inequality $|
abla\phi_R|^2\leqslant C\phi_R$, to get \[ |\Gamma'_R(t)|\leqslant 2\sqrt{2E(u)}\Big(\int|
abla\phi_R|^2|u|^2\Big)^{1/2} \leqslant C\sqrt{E(u_0)}\sqrt{\Gamma_R(t)}. \] By integrating between a fixed $t\in[0,T)$ and $t_n$, we obtain, \[ |\sqrt{\Gamma_R(t)}-\sqrt{\Gamma_R(t_n)}|\leqslant C|t-t_n|. \] Applying (ef{c}) in Step 2, we get \[ \Gamma_R(t_n)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^N} |u_n(x)|^2\phi_R(x)dxightarrow M_\mathrm{gs}^2\phi_R(0)=0. \] Thus, letting $n	o\infty$ in the last inequality, we obtain, for all $t\in [0,T)$ and all $R>0$, \[ \Gamma_R(t) \leqslant C(T-t)^2. \] Since the right-hand side of the last expression is independent of $R$, letting $R	o\infty$ yields, for all $t\in [0,T)$, \[ u(t)\in\Sigma \quad	ext{and}\quad 0\leqslant \Gamma(t) \leqslant C(T-t)^2. \] From this estimate, we can extend by continuity $\Gamma(t)$ at $t=T$ by setting $\Gamma(T)=0$, from which we also obtain $\Gamma'(T)=0$. Moreover, since $u(t)\in\Sigma$ and $u$ is a solution of~\eqref{inls}, we may apply Lemma~ef{virial.lemma}, and by (ef{virialthree}) we obtain $\Gamma''(t)=16E(u_0)$, which finally gives, for all $t\in [0,T)$, \[ \Gamma(t) = 8E(u_0)(T-t)^2. \] Letting $t=0$, we find, using identity (ef{virialone}) \[ \Gamma(0)=\int |x|^2|u_0|^2= 8E(u_0)T^2 \quad 	ext{and}\quad \Gamma'(0)= 4\int x\cdot\mathrm{Im}(\overline{u_0}
abla u_0) = -16E(u_0)T. \] \medskip 
oindent {\em Step 4.} We apply identity \eqref{valeria} with $u_0$, $s= rac{1}{2T}$, and $	heta(x)= rac{|x|^2}{2}$ to get \[  egin{split} E(u_0e^{i rac{|x|^2}{4T}})&=E(u_0)+ rac{1}{2T}\int x\cdot	ext{Im}( ar{u_0}
abla u_0) + rac{1}{8T^2}\int|x|^2|u_0|^2\ &=E(u_0)+ rac{1}{2T}(-4E(u_0))+ rac{1}{8T^2}(8E(u_0)T^2)=0. \end{split} \] Note that this calculation justifies, {\em a posteriori}, the application of~\eqref{valeria} with $	heta(x)= rac{|x|^2}{2}
ot\in C_0^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^N)$. Since $\|u_0e^{i rac{|x|^2}{4T}}\|_{L^2}=M_\mathrm{gs}$ and $E(u_0e^{i rac{|x|^2}{4T}})=0$, we can deduce from Proposition ef{charact.prop} that there exist $\lambda_1>0$, $\gamma_1\in\mathbb{R}$, and $\widetilde{Q}\in\mathcal{G}$ such that \[ u_0(x)=e^{i\gamma_1}e^{-i rac{|x|^2}{4T}}\lambda_1^{N/2}\widetilde{Q}(\lambda_1x). \] Finally, we use the pseudo-conformal transformation. We define $	ilde\lambda_0=\lambda_1T>0$ and $	ilde\gamma_0=\gamma_1-\lambda_1^2T\in\mathbb{R}$, and write $u_0$ as \[ u_0(x)=e^{i	ilde\gamma_0}e^{i rac{	ilde\lambda^2_0}{T}} e^{-i rac{|x|^2}{4T}}\Big( rac{	ilde\lambda_0}{T}\Big)^{N/2}\widetilde{Q}\Big( rac{	ilde\lambda_0x}{T}\Big). \] Thus, $u_0=S_{\widetilde{Q},T,	ilde\lambda_0,	ilde\gamma_0}(0)$, where $S_{\widetilde{Q},T,	ilde\lambda_0,	ilde\gamma_0}$ is defined by (ef{S2.def}). By invoking uniqueness of the solution of (ef{inls}), we find that $u(t)=S_{\widetilde{Q},T,	ilde\lambda_0,	ilde\gamma_0}(t)$ for all $t\in[0,T)$, which concludes the proof.

